 "limits": [
  "a literal string is not an access: the 'files naming it' column counts spellings, so a docstring line that says 'verdict' raises the base rate without any code reading a key",
  "the subscript arm matches only a string-literal slice; a computed key (k = 'verdict'; D[k]) is invisible to it by construction and is counted separately as computed_subscripts rather than folded into the zero",
  "the class is a base rate, not a lineage test: generic_word says a hit needs lineage evidence before it means anything, not that any particular hit is a collision",
  "files over 1MB are skipped and counted as a hole (E391's cap), so a very large module that spells a key is absent from its denominator while present in any consumer that reads the file directly",
  "the receiver root is the leftmost Name of the container, so `self.report['rows']` is published under 'self' and two different objects under one name share a row",
  "the counted read lines are the ones E638's join publishes; a key spelled in a module that holds no counted read is counted in files_naming_it only"
 ],
 "verdict": "the six counted row keys price differently: at least one is a generic word and at least one is private or narrow, so a key-name hit has to be priced per key rather than over the set",
 "answer": "5 of 6 counted row keys are generic words (>=20 files outside the counted faces), 0 are shared but narrow, 1 are private to the counted faces. The widest is 'verdict' (3473 files, 3657 read sites) and the narrowest is 'slip_exec' (0 files, 5 read sites), so a single key-name hit is worth most on 'slip_exec' and least on 'verdict'."
}
